<title>GERMANY: Germans oppose euro by two-to-one margin - survey.</title>
<headline>Germans oppose euro by two-to-one margin - survey.</headline>
<dateline>BONN 1997-06-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>Two out of three Germans oppose the planned single European currency, but four in five said they expect the euro to soon replace the deutschemark, a survey published on Friday said.</p>
<p>The ZDF television's monthly Politbarometer survey found 62 percent of Germans were against the euro compared to 31 percent who said they thought it was &quot;good&quot; that the single currency would be introduced.</p>
<p>The survey of 1,332 Germans conducted by the Electoral Research Group found that 82 percent now believe the euro's introduction is inevitable, the highest number of voters ever recorded in the survey who believed the euro would be launched.</p>
<p>Only 15 percent said they do not expect the euro to go ahead.</p>
</text>
